macy running out  that american certainty about a russian  invasion seems to be backed up by events  unfolding on the ground tonight russian  media reports that a major gas pipeline  in eastern ukraine has suffered some  sort of explosion and at the same time  jeff the u.s is all but certain that  russia is to blame for a series of cyber  attacks this week that took down  ukrainian banks and government websites  jackson proskow in washington tonight  thanks jackson  and with that threat of an invasion on  the rise poland is now preparing for a  sudden rush of ukrainians to the border  tens of thousands of people could be  displaced in the event of a russian  attack our europe bureau chief crystal  gamansing is in poland tonight and has  more on the planned humanitarian  response  tucked away in an industrial park a  symbol recognized around the world  in the city of lublin in eastern poland  you'll find the red cross's largest base  in the country about two hours from the  border with ukraine  it's the polish government leading plans  to care for ukrainians should they need  to flee west  christian  i want to stress that we are ready to  help those who will be forced to leave  ukraine  the red cross anticipates there could be  as many as 50 000 people crossing into  poland per day should russia invade  the compounding effect could create a  humanitarian crisis on a tremendous  scale  while there's still lots of work to be  done the agency has some items in  holding from 2021 when there was a  crisis on the polish belarussian border  many refugees mostly from the middle  east were misled by officials in belarus  into thinking poland was an open entry  point into europe there are backpacks  and blankets and and cots and and some  of these items will actually be  repurposed for any ukrainians who happen  to cross the border  at the first stage there will be  refugees camps organized near the border  refugees will receive a medical and  social aid however those people must be  transported as fast as possible deeply  into poland territory to midland and to  the west because those people will not  be able to live for one week in  gymnasium  relocating those seeking refuge could  help poland more easily absorb the large  numbers of people  a similar strategy was used in 2014 when  fighting first broke out in the east of  ukraine between pro-russian separatists  and ukrainians polish government  officials say they're being guided by  the country's crisis management plans  and those will evolve based on the  situation on the ground  bottom line ukrainians will be welcomed  jeff  crystal comancing reporting in poland  tonight thanks  at least 11 people are now missing after  a ferry off the coast of greece burst  into flames the ship was about three  hours into its journey to italy more  than 200 people have been rescued  including a couple of truck drivers who  had to be airlifted off of the ferry the  cause of that fire is now under  investigation  canada's mounting metal count still  ahead more olympic glory for canadians  in beijing  [Music]  you're watching global national  [Music]  the race is on for the last remaining  medals in beijing with the winter games  wrapping up on sunday and overnight two  canadian freestyle skiers landed on the  half pipe podium at the same time  defending champion bc's cassie sharp won  the silver medal while ontario's rachel  karker bagged the bronze making her  olympic debut one to remember it's  cassie sharp's second straight olympic  medal after winning gold in pyeongchang  in 2018 and she did it just one year  after undergoing major knee surgery  and team canada is also back on the  podium in men's curling newfoundland and  labrador skip brad gushu steered his  canadian rink to the bronze medal  defeating defending olympic champions  the united states eight to five  and it was redemption for a determined  laurent dubrey the quebec native skated  to a surprise silver medal in the men's  1000 meter